Subject: Scratch out your eyes, apologize. 

In <9312071527.AA25591@mx.nsi.nasa.gov>, GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov>writes:

>>Never admit to your apponent in a debate that he is right and you are 
wrong....

In a debate where you are scoring points and the object is to get points,
this may be reasonable.  In an intelligent conversation where the object
is to deduce the truth or to learn, this is a poor procedure.

Pure debates with judging on style is rare.  In most "debates" where
there is social or political pressure to win "points," people are
supposedly looking for the truth while actually counting points.  In
these cases, you lose your style points if an audience thinks you're
wrong but can't see it yourself.  In such cases, it is best to quickly
admit the errror and adjust your argument.  Unless your totally wrong
about every essential point in your position, a single error won't effect 
the outcome much.

This latter situation is usually the world of work.  Your bosses and 
clients assess you politically on "points" because they usually don't have 
the expertese to follow the technical details.  If the think you are wrong 
on details but continue to argue, they lose your trust or respect.  I find 
the pure debate where style is more important than truth to be nonexistent 
in the real world.
