> in Chapter 2: "Assymetries: Women and Men Talking at Cross-Purposes", I 
> found a number of glaring stereotypes that I wish she wouldn't propogate.
> Such as: 1) Woman being OK w/asking for directions, and Man not wanting
> to, 2) Woman having difficulty w/mechanical things like cars and computers.

I have found both of those "stereotypes" to be quite commonly seen in
real life. I think it in general true that many women don't understand the
problem many men have (myself included) in showing any kind of mental
or physical deficit, even over something as simple as asking directions.
And, while there are some notable exceptions, most of the women I know
are unequivocal in stating their general discomfort with mechanical things.
(This doesn't mean they're helpless with a VCR, just that they themselves
will tell you that they don't feel confident about using them.)

It's interesting that you dislike Tannen's explanations for being too
"anecdotal," because she claims that that's a common complaint made by
men about women's arguing technique. In fact, I'd like to suggest, without
any attempt to flatter, that you are an exceptional woman, Amara, and
as such, find all of these generalizations hopelessly off the mark. Were
you more ordinary you might not disagree with them so strongly.

Which leads me to an explanation of my own way of thinking about how
the so-called "innate gender differences" really work in human
behaviour:  I think of the possible range of human behaviour as a
surface in some imaginary n-space. All of the things a person could do
are represented by a point somewhere on that surface. The effect of 3
billion years of evolution has been to put some depressions in the
surface, areas of lower energy where its "easier" to go. Some of them
are tiny dents, like the urge to scratch an itch, and some of them are
huge troughs or incredible canyons (e.g. the need for sleep). In
comparison, socialization makes a numer of tiny scratches in the
surface, but these can be seen as well.  Somewhere on that surface are
depressions for "typical" male or female behaviour. The actions of an
individual can be thought of as one or more marbles moving along this
surface, occasionally getting trapped in various depressions, rolling
out of them, etc. This much could be said for any organism. What's
special about homo sapiens is the extent to which conscious thought can
be used to avoid the depressions in the surface, or climb out of them.
Naturally, the level of this ability differs between individuals.

So, when someone talks about generalizations of some subset of
humanity, what they're trying to capture is (hopefully) the shape of
that surface.  They should understand that the shape of the surface is
not necessarily a mandate for a given individual, or even for large
groups of them.  On the other hand, people who easily surmount these
barriers tend to downplay them or not recognize them at all.
 

