cpresson@ingr.com (Craig Presson) writes ...

>In <199312071719.JAA06864@well.sf.ca.us>, Brian D Williams writes:
>|> 
>|>  Tim May has hit this one right on the head! What a great idea, and the
>|> perfect time! Maybe we can get the NRA to sponsor it! (reaching for the
>|> phone)
>|> 
>They already are! Check the latest _American Rifleman/Hunter_. 1, 2, 3
>strikes you're out at the old Con Game :-)

Hmm.

Much as I object to repeat killings, I don't necessarily agree that
this would be a good idea. 

As an aside, I also object to the death penalty; not because it's cruel
or unusual, but because it's _irreversible_.  My best estimate is that
5-10% of the people jailed for life in the UK for murder in the past 20
years have been miscarriages of justice.  A lot of them have been
released and, to some extent, compensated for long periods of
unjustifiable imprisonment.  If the death penalty had been retained
they'd have been hanged, and would be beyond any compensation.

(I'm not an absolutist on the death penality; my objection falls if
Alcor et al ever succeed in reviving someone who's been cryonically
suspended.  Then you could leave murderers in a vat of LN2 for a
century, tip them out if no mitigating evidence comes to light, or
revive them if it was later discovered that there'd been a miscarriage
of justice.)

I'm inclined to suggest that campaigning for a life sentence that works
would be more appropriate than campaigning for an elaborate system
that is engineered to let people repeatedly re-offend then kill them
with great ceremony and expense. Here in the UK there is no set term
for a life sentence (standard, indeed mandatory, for murder). _Most_
life prisoners are released on license after between 14 and 20 years,
but _some_ are still behind bars after 30, and it's an open secret
that a few (like Peter Sutcliffe, or Brady and Hindley) will only
leave prison in a coffin. And even after a lifer's released, the
license can be revoked for _any_ infraction (even a parking ticket).
It's for life, and it means life. And the re-offense rate is extremely
low, because people who're deemed likely to re-offend just don't get
released. 

Note that the average cost of a state execution in the US was put at
about a million dollars by one program that I saw; the cost of keeping
a prisoner in jail is about $50,000 a year. So it's not, in principle,
much more expensive to run a British-style "serious" life imprisonment
system than to execute felons. 
 
Now seriously: I'm startled to see extropians discussing something
that I consider to be the ultimate statist atrocity -- giving the bastards
the _automatic_, _mandatory_ authority to execute people. Doesn't this
strike any of you as incongruous? Here we are talking about boundless
optimism and maximizing personal potential, and then suddenly the
debate's moved onto bleating excuses to give the state more powers of
life or death over us!

I'd like to see an end to this thread as fast as possible, or at least
a turn in a more extropian direction.  How about discussing this:
what kind of effective criminal sanctions would be appropriate to a
PPL system when the damage caused by the assailant is greater than
their total lifetime earning potential plus their insurance cover?



-- Charlie
