	I mentioned this book by Harvard philosphy professor Robert Nozick (has
written other books like "The Examined Self" and "Anarchy and State") in a
previous post some while ago and no one seems to have noticed it.  In fact, I
mentioned I am a newcomer to the list and the Welcome Wagon seems to have lost
its way.  Perhaps some of the grousing about a stale list has to do with stale
common courtesy.  Maybe a small factor.

	Paul Whitmore was commenting on the paradox of knowledge vs. power and I
would direct him to this book which discussed rational belief generation,
formulation of principles to live by and current decision theory research (as
well as some interesting heuristics for rationally thinking about novel
concepts).

	If anyone else has read it, I would like to share my concern that Nozick
has included what he calls Symbolic Utility along with Consequential Utility and
Evidential Utility into Decision Value calculations.  To me, this is a
dangerous, seductive road to travel down.  It awkwardly places value and fact
together into rational decision making.  Symbolic Utility generally boils down
to value (morality) while evidence and consequence stick cleanly with previously
defined rationality.  Claiming that Symbolic Utility is used to make rational
decisions is giving a rationality license to liberals and theists (I know,
redundancy).  This is dangerous for we must continue to correctly identify
liberalism and supernaturalism (I know, more redundancy) as irrational.  I would
be interested to hear some thoughts on this.

	Morality and rationality cannot be neatly tied together in an equation.
They are far more messy.  You may be able to rationally arrive at a moral goal,
but you are left hanging onto nothing when it comes to declaring the moral goal
rational by itself.  Likewise, you may be able to morally arrive at a rational
goal, but you are left hanging onto nothing when it comes to declaring the
rational goal moral by itself.

