As someone who has worked his whole (arguably) adult life in publishing 
(some magazine, books--literature and technical) I have to take issue 
with Charlie Stross over his statement

>>Something is wrong with the publishing industry. It's in a spiral of
lowering standards, increasing breadth, and decreasing profitability.
The smart money is on finding a new creative outlet of equivalent
durability, artistic merit, and superior profitability (on which to
live and create more art, etcetera). 

The publishing industry, like television, should not be taken to task for 
what the market will support. As someone who regularly receives (not 
large) paychecks from that industry, I can tell you that the idea of 
publishing "high standard" (which I assume is a euphemism for "writerly") 
books is a boorishly simple sentiment.  Would Intel engineers take a 25% 
cut in pay because a popular new chip they were designing was scratched 
because it was inelegant from an engineers perspective? How many copies 
of Coriolanus have you bought this year? Have the gift books you've doled 
out in the holiday season been new voices in poetry or something closer 
to "A Bloom County Omnibus?"

Further, who are we to judge? If a million people read only one book in a 
year and that book is Howard Stern's "Private Parts" who are we to say 
that their enjoyment is any less valid, vital or enriching than that 
taken from another reading "The Feynman Lectures,"  Ayn Rand or  your own 
fiction? 

Lowering standards? "Penny Dreadfuls" was not a term invented in the 
current century.  The scary part about the average reader is that, by 
definition, roughly 50% of the population is *less* sophisticated than 
him/her.

"Pay's Rubbish?" So what's your point? Do you want to write or do you 
want to make money? Do you think the exodus of Faulkner, Fitzgerald, 
Steinbeck, et. al.,  to Hollywood (and in some cases from there to 
alcoholic collapse) was because their very well-known names were securing 
them a lot of money from a pre-television public who read (and thus 
presumably supported them) a whole lot more? For people being paid 
rubbish, it's nice company to keep.

Not making a lot of money is the price you pay for practicing an archaic 
art form. Check out your local musician, painter or poet. The majority of 
those making a living from their work (dropping the superstar category, 
which is too rare to be a factor) are those who are *surprise* producing 
day in and out. That would translate to more than one story in the past 
year. I know, I know you mentioned a novel, and that's great but you 
can't sell what you don't submit. 

>>Anyone got any ideas for media which can be worked in with minimal
investment by a solitary specialist? I'm all ears.

As someone who is keeping his day job while attempting to be published 
himself, I certainly empathize  with the meager rewards and daunting 
obstacles that Charlie is facing. But writers are unique in their 
willingness to sit around and whine about how hard their lot in being a 
writer is. Would you want an airline pilot/brain surgeon who had made a 
"minimal investment" in training? 

Amateur writers (I'm *not* referring to Charlie) love to define their 
"craft" as a "gift", something immune to examination or effort. But most 
of writing (like any skill) is plain (and ugly) hard work that most don't 
want to do. If you want to write, Charlie, do it for the love of it. 
Whether or not the money follows is really in the hands of fate. But 
don't blame the messenger, publishing, for the message.



